  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThis afternoon into tonight:\nLow pressure is passing through Iowa and southern Minnesota\ntoday with the bulk of the attendant synoptic rain to our south.\nWe have had more thunderstorm activity up here this morning due\nto some convergence and a small amount of instability. That has\nresulted in some pretty good rainfall totals in the 1-3 inch\nrange for a few areas, but this hasn't necessarily been\nwidespread. Convection has really dropped off recently and we're\nleft with mostly a broad area of light to moderate rain with a\nfew embedded heavier showers. There hasn't been any lightning\nout there in a couple hours. With very limited instability now,\nthis will likely be the case for another few hours early this\nafternoon. Later on, on the northern edge of the synoptic rain,\nwe may expect a bit of redevelopment of some thunderstorms in\nnorthwest Wisconsin with some afternoon heating. Models still\nwant to redevelop a few showers and perhaps storms along and\naround the North Shore as well, owing to some midlevel\nconvergence. Any storms will remain sub-severe, and flooding\nconcerns at this point are very minimal. Rainfall rates have at\ntimes been pretty impressive this morning, but with current\nrates winding down, even a brief uptick in rates this afternoon\nis not likely to cause much issues. Expect low pressure to move\noff to the east tonight, and any lingering showers and storms\nshould end this evening from west to east. \n\nSaturday through Tuesday:\nRidging develops on Saturday aloft with drier air filtering in.\nThere could be a few showers that develop Saturday afternoon in\nor around the Arrowhead with a weak embedded shortwave passing\nby aloft and some surface convergence possible as we get an\neast-northeast lake breeze and more northerly winds inland.\nTemperature-wise, we get more seasonable with highs in the 70s\nto low 80s. The 70s are expected around Lake Superior with\nonshore flow.\n\nThe onshore flow will persist into Sunday around Lake Superior,\nbut inland, wind direction will switch to more east to\nsoutheasterly as surface high pressure to our north moves east\nand we get some return flow. Sunday's highs will be pretty\nsimilar to Saturday, but nighttime lows in north-central\nMinnesota start to climb a bit with warm air advection\ndeveloping as we go into Monday. \n\nWidespread low 80s are looking likely on Monday with pretty\nuniform southerly flow except onshore right by Lake Superior. A\ncold front is expected to move through Tuesday, and ahead of\nthat Monday night, showers and thunderstorms are likely to\ndevelop. At this time, it's looking most likely that any severe\nthreat will be over in eastern North Dakota into northwest\nMinnesota based on timing and diurnal instability. If that trend\nchanges such that the front moves faster, that could put a\nlittle of our area (e.g. Brainerd Lakes towards International\nFalls) at a greater risk for seeing a few stronger storms. In\ngeneral, we might have potential for up to 1.5 kJ/kg of MUCAPE\nand 30-40 kt 0-6 km shear which could promote a few strong to\nsevere storms, but more likely is that this threat will be\nlargely to our west and we will get the leftovers with some rain\nand general thunderstorms moving through Monday night. Strong\nwinds and perhaps some hail will be the main threats if any\nstronger storms were to happen, but it's a pretty low (<10%)\nchance at this time.\n\nShowers and storms will gradually continue east on Tuesday,\nlingering into the Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin\npotentially. Cooler temperatures with highs in the 70s are\nexpected on Tuesday following the front. \n\nWednesday through Friday: \nAnother relatively cool and quiet day on Wednesday with highs\nlikely in the 70s. Flow aloft is looking like it will become\nmore quasi-zonal with several passing waves. We may have some\nmore rain chances around Thursday into Thursday night, then\nperhaps again around Friday into Friday night.",
